WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.830
You need to email a huge project file.

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.870
It's far too big to send.

3
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.510
So you compress it.

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.810
It shrinks, zips across the
internet, and opens on their laptop.

5
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.970
Compare it with the original, bit by bit.

6
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.630
Every bit matches.

7
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.310
Smaller, yet nothing has changed.

8
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.470
How can a file shrink but
still open exactly as before?

9
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.750
In this mission, you'll see
why compression matters:

10
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.450
smaller files need less
storage and less time to send.

11
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.350
Then you'll zoom into a file's
data, like a simple logo.

12
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.850
And you'll notice something:
data often repeats.

13
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.390
Pixel after pixel of
exactly the same blue.

14
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.840
Your mission: explain how a file can
shrink but still open exactly as before.
